const articleParagraphs = [
  ["Start with identity, not perfection", "The first job of a QC review is to confirm that the warehouse received the item you intended to order. Compare the product type, selected color, size label, obvious construction details and included parts with the source listing. Do not begin by hunting for microscopic defects. If the item itself, colorway or selected size is wrong, small quality details no longer matter. Keep the original listing open beside the warehouse photos and work through the evidence in the same order every time."],
  ["Check whether the photo set is sufficient", "A useful QC set should show the item from enough angles to support a decision. For footwear, that normally means both sides, the front, heel, outsole, size label and an overhead view. For clothing, look for the front, back, tags, print or embroidery, cuffs, seams and measurements when sizing is important. One attractive image is not evidence of consistent construction. If a critical area is hidden, blurred or outside the frame, request a focused photograph before international shipping."],
  ["Read shape before tiny details", "Overall shape often exposes problems faster than zooming into a logo. Place paired items on the same visual line and compare height, toe shape, panel position, sleeve length or pocket alignment. Look for asymmetry that remains visible across more than one photo. Perspective can distort a single frame, so confirm a suspected issue using another angle. A difference that disappears when the camera moves is more likely to be perspective than construction."],
  ["Use reference lines for alignment", "Straight edges make alignment easier to judge. Compare printed graphics with the center line of a shirt, eyelets with the edge of a shoe, pockets with side seams and labels with nearby stitching. The warehouse floor grid, ruler or measurement tape can help, but only when it sits in the same plane as the item. A ruler floating above a curved surface can create a misleading reading. Ask for a flat measurement if a dimension affects fit or shipping."],
  ["Separate cosmetic variation from structural defects", "Minor loose threads, removable dust and small packaging marks are not the same as broken hardware, open seams, deep scratches or missing components. Decide in advance which defects would make you return an item. This prevents an emotional decision after you have already waited for the warehouse. Structural issues, incorrect specifications and damage that could worsen during shipping deserve more weight than tiny variations only visible under heavy zoom."],
  ["Treat color cautiously", "Warehouse lighting, camera white balance, screen settings and reflective materials can all change apparent color. Compare multiple photos and use neutral objects in the frame as a rough reference. If the listing offers several similar colors, verify the selected variant name or code instead of relying only on your screen. When exact color matters, request a daylight photograph or an image beside a neutral gray reference if the service offers that option."],
  ["Measure the places that control fit", "A size tag confirms what was packed, not how the item fits. For tops, chest width and length are usually more useful than the label alone. For trousers, waist, rise, inseam and leg opening may matter. For shoes, an insole measurement is more informative than an outsole estimate. Compare measurements with an item you already own and measure that reference using the same method. Different measurement methods can create false differences of several centimeters."],
  ["Use weight and dimensions as shipping evidence", "Product weight and packed dimensions affect international shipping, especially when volumetric weight applies. Bulky boxes can cost more than the item itself. Treat warehouse weight as an estimate until packaging choices are confirmed, and distinguish item weight from parcel weight. If you plan to remove boxes, consolidate items or add protective packaging, the final parcel may differ from the single-item record. Review the shipping line rules again when the parcel is ready."],
  ["Compare repeated records, not one lucky sample", "One good QC set proves only that one unit looked acceptable. When several records exist for the same source item, compare recurring details: stitching consistency, print placement, color, packaging and dispatch speed. Repeated patterns are more useful than one unusually good or bad example. Still verify your own warehouse photos, because seller batches and inventory can change. Historical records are references, not guarantees."],
  ["Make the final decision explicit", "End every review with one of four actions: ship, request another check, exchange or return. Write down the reason in one sentence. For example: ship because identity, measurements and visible construction match expectations; request another photo because the heel is hidden; exchange because the size label is wrong; return because hardware is damaged. A clear decision record prevents you from reopening the same question and helps if you later need to explain a problem."],
  ["What this guide cannot guarantee", "QC photos reduce uncertainty; they do not prove authenticity, durability, legal importability or final delivery condition. Images cannot reveal every material property or hidden construction issue. Shipping restrictions, customs treatment, seller policies, prices and inventory can change. Always verify the live listing, current service rules and the laws that apply to your destination before spending money. FindQC is an independent research interface and does not sell, inspect or ship products."],
];

function esc(value) {
  return String(value).replace(/[&<>'"]/g, (char) => ({ "&": "&amp;", "<": "&lt;", ">": "&gt;", "'": "&#39;", '"': "&quot;" }[char]));
}

function parsePath(pathname) {
  const parts = pathname.split("/").filter(Boolean);
  const lang = languages[parts[0]] ? parts.shift() : "en";
  return { lang, path: `/${parts.join("/")}` || "/" };
}

function localizedPath(lang, path) {
  if (path === "/") return lang === "en" ? "/" : `/${lang}`;
  return `${lang === "en" ? "" : `/${lang}`}${path}`;
}

function languageMenu(lang, path) {
  return Object.entries(languages).map(([code, item]) => `<a class="language ${code === lang ? "active" : ""}" href="${localizedPath(code, path)}" hreflang="${code}">${item.label}</a>`).join("");
}

